    "imageName": "LessingDict_Page_0483.jpg",
    "explain": "xè n. a) Foot, leg. deger-e bosxu. To rise to one's feet (also metaph.). deger-e inu yaryaxu. To put somebody on his feet. yar. Hands and feet, extremities. kønggen. Light-footed; quick to render services. neicegylyn jabuxu. To keep in step with. saitai. Sturdy-legged. xurdun. Light-footed; swift-footed; quick to render services. KOL ygei: Footless. See also (b). yn bayubci. Anklet. -- yn .mør. Footstep, trace. -- yn orijalta. Cloth wrappers worn under boots. zigiiky. To stretch the legs. kimel -- . Artificial foot or leg. ojudal un -- masin-a. Treadle sewing machine. uridu -- . Foreleg or foot. b) Something resembling or functioning as a leg or foot ( of a natural or man-made object); foot, leg, base, stand, lower part of an object, etc. ygei. Bottomless; very deep. See also (a). ygei usu. Bottomless body of water. ayula jin -- . The foot of a mountain. gyr yn --. Piers of a bridge. naran u --. Point where the sun sets. siregen y -- . Leg of a table. usun u --. Bottom of a body of water (such as river, lake, or ocean). ziruy un aparat un -- . Tripod of a photographic camera. c) Calamity, upheaval, trouble; hardship. -- yimegen. Disturbance, riot. cerig yn --. War troubles. dain bailduyan u --. Hardships of war. daisun -- : Bandits, highwaymen. d) Special and idiomatic uses. bolxu. To bustle. dy oruxu. To begin to walk (of a baby). inu yazar kyrky ygei. To feel as if walking on air (from joy). kyndy. Pregnant, pregnancy. meden xaraixu. To jump or rush at full speed. mori. Wheel horse, shaft-horse. nigedky. To be an accomplice or an associate in guilt. -- zalyaxu . To give to the needy; to help materially those who ask. em yn --. See under em. γudamzi jin --. Street traffic. mal un --. The mumber of livestock. yge jin -- . A line (in a poem, verse, probably after S. pada).",
